<td id="S4.T3.7.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.8.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4.1575</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.8.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.8.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3.8039</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.8.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.8.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3.8721</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.7.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.7.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S4.T3.7.9.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   w/o entity-separated</span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.7.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.9.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4.1294</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.9.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4.1587</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.9.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3.8100</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.9.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.9.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3.8693</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.7.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.7.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S4.T3.7.10.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   w/o saliency head</span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.7.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.10.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4.0775</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.10.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4.0979</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.10.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3.7851</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.10.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.10.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3.8491</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.7.11" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.7.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><span id="S4.T3.7.11.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   w/o temporal mask</span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.7.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.11.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4.1405</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.11.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4.1658</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.11.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3.8153</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.11.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.7.11.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3.8860</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T3.7.12" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T3.7.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.7.12.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   w/ temporal decay <math id="S4.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=10</annotation></semantics></math></span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.7.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.7.12.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4.1385</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.7.12.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">4.1572</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.12.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.7.12.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3.8315</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.7.12.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.7.12.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">3.8981</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Saliency construction.</span> The saliency head is the largest single contributor: removing it and falling back to an NCE-only variant produces the largest drop on both axes. Ablating InfoNCE hurts both axes, with a larger drop on motion, consistent with the Stage 1 ablation (Fig. <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Quantitative metrics: definitions and rationale. ‣ A.3 Stage 1 saliency aligner: ablation grid and quantitative analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, Tab. <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.5 Stage 2 saliency on the full MTSS caption ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) where <em id="S4.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o NCE</em> pushes mass onto the dominant subject, suppressing subject-background relations. Replacing the <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+2</annotation></semantics></math> entity-separated forwards with a single union-mask forward collapses <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> into one foreground blob and also hurts both axes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Pair-routing operator and temporal weighting.</span> Among the three operators in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.4 Stage 2: Saliency-routed TRD ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), OR dominates: XOR keeps only FG-BG boundary pairs and drops the FG-FG structural relations, while AND trails OR because it discards FG-BG grounding. MoAlign induces an AND-like bias via its motion subspace and lands close to (saliency, AND) but below (saliency, OR). Removing the saliency mask from the cross-frame term (<em id="S4.SS5.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o temporal mask</em>) also trails full SARA, so the cross-frame term benefits from saliency-weighted pair selection. Adding MoAlign-style decay (<math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau{=}10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau{=}10</annotation></semantics></math>) on top of SARA does not help, confirming that gains come from saliency routing rather than temporal weighting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These ablations pin SARA’s gain to two components: a calibrated text-conditioned saliency in Stage 1, and an OR pair-routing operator in Stage 2 that keeps both subject-subject structure and subject-background grounding. Remove either one, or replace OR with AND/XOR, and SARA falls back towards the existing TRD baselines.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SARA reframes semantic guidance for VDM representation alignment as a pair-routing problem on top of token-relation distillation. A lightweight Stage 1 aligner trained with per-entity SAM 3.1 masks and an InfoNCE regulariser predicts a continuous text-conditioned saliency, which is fused into TRD at Stage 2 through an OR pair-routing operator. This reallocates supervision from background-background pairs toward subject-subject and subject-background relations, while leaving the TRD form, V-JEPA target, and trainable diffusion-path parameter count unchanged. Under a matched experimental setup, SARA consistently outperforms diverse baseline methods across all evaluations.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Visualisations</h2>

<figure id="A1.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07800v1/case1.png" id="A1.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/535;" width="476" height="535" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="A1.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative comparison on a representative caption. The key point highlighted is the colors of the liquids in the two kettles and the cups. SARA produces videos that correctly render the specified visual attributes, whereas baselines exhibit attribute mis-binding or omission.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07800v1/case2.png" id="A1.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/508;" width="476" height="508" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="A1.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Qualitative comparison on a multi-entity scene with six people and two pairs of shoes. The key points are correctly rendering two distinct pairs of sneakers (red Nike and blue-red) and all six people. SARA faithfully generates both objects and all persons, while baselines miss entities or confuse object attributes.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section collects qualitative evidence for SARA’s Stage 1 design and Stage 2 generalisation. App. <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 SAM 3.1 entity decomposition (Stage 1 supervision target) ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> shows the per-entity SAM 3.1 supervision target <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>. App. <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Stage 1 saliency aligner: ablation grid and quantitative analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> ablates the Stage 1 recipe on two held-out clips, with matching quantitative metrics in Tab. <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.5 Stage 2 saliency on the full MTSS caption ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. App. <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Stage 1 saliency on per-entity queries ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a> traces the inference-time saliency through the four supervision-time queries on a held-out two-person clip, and App. <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Stage 2 saliency on the full MTSS caption ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a> queries the same aligner with the full MTSS caption used at Stage 2. App. <a href="#A1.SS6" title="A.6 Qualitative video samples ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.6</span></a> reserves side-by-side video comparisons against baselines for the project page.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>SAM 3.1 entity decomposition (Stage 1 supervision target)</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ A.1 SAM 3.1 entity decomposition (Stage 1 supervision target) ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> unpacks one training clip into the five binary masks that Stage 1 supervises against (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Stage 1: Text-conditioned saliency aligner ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>): one per-entity mask <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> per Reference item, the foreground union <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{fg}}=\bigcup_{k}M_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{fg}}=\bigcup_{k}M_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the complement <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{bg}}=\mathbf{1}-M_{\text{fg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>bg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{bg}}=\mathbf{1}-M_{\text{fg}}</annotation></semantics></math>, all produced offline by the simplifier-driven SAM 3.1 pipeline of App. <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Linking MTSS entities to SAM 3.1 masks ‣ Appendix B MTSS captioning and entity-mask preparation ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>. The clip illustrates two properties that carry over to the saliency design: (i) the three foreground entities span very different scales (a small held card vs. a partially off-frame second person), so a single union-mask forward would let the dominant entity erase the small ones, which motivates the <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+2</annotation></semantics></math> entity-separated forwards, and (ii) <span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PERSON_2</span>’s mask still tracks the partially off-frame body, so SAM 3.1’s open-vocabulary text prompting recovers entities even under partial framing.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07800v1/sam3_entity_masks.png" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:390/237;" width="390" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F5.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="A1.F5.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">SAM 3.1 entity decomposition used as Stage 1 supervision.<span id="A1.F5.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Top row: input frames. Next three rows: per-entity masks for <span id="A1.F5.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">OBJECT_1</span> (red), <span id="A1.F5.10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PERSON_1</span> (green), <span id="A1.F5.10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PERSON_2</span> (blue). Last two rows: complement <span id="A1.F5.10.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BACKGROUND</span> (<math id="A1.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{bg}}=\mathbf{1}-M_{\text{fg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>bg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{bg}}=\mathbf{1}-M_{\text{fg}}</annotation></semantics></math>, yellow) and foreground union <span id="A1.F5.10.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ALL Entities</span> (<math id="A1.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{fg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{fg}}</annotation></semantics></math>, magenta). All five masks supervise the saliency head jointly via <math id="A1.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+2</annotation></semantics></math> forwards (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Stage 1: Text-conditioned saliency aligner ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>).</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Pair-budget analysis</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.2 Pair-budget analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> reports the empirical pair-budget breakdown on <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{,}400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{,}400</annotation></semantics></math> training clips (<math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="76{,}800" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>76,800</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">76{,}800</annotation></semantics></math> frames). The foreground fraction <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{fg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{fg}}</annotation></semantics></math> is derived from per-entity SAM 3.1 masks (the same masks that supervise Stage 1). As Fig. <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.2 Pair-budget analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>(a) shows, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{fg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{fg}}</annotation></semantics></math> centres around <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.48</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.48</annotation></semantics></math> with moderate variance across clips. Under uniform pair weighting (i.e. VideoREPA), the expected share of each pair category in the <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> TRD budget is determined by <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{fg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{fg}}</annotation></semantics></math>: subject–subject pairs receive <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{fg}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mtext>fg</mtext><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{fg}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, subject–background pairs <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\,p_{\text{fg}}(1-p_{\text{fg}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\,p_{\text{fg}}(1-p_{\text{fg}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and background–background pairs <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-p_{\text{fg}})^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-p_{\text{fg}})^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fig. <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ A.2 Pair-budget analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>(b) visualises the resulting budget split under each routing operator. The empirical numbers confirm that background–background pairs consume roughly <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the budget while subject–subject pairs receive only <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\!26\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>26</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\!26\%</annotation></semantics></math>, motivating the pair-routing operators of Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Stage 2: Saliency-routed TRD ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.07800v1/pair_budget.svg" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/168;" width="405" height="168"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F6.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="A1.F6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Pair-budget breakdown on the training corpus<span id="A1.F6.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (<math id="A1.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{,}400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{,}400</annotation></semantics></math> clips, <math id="A1.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="76{,}800" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>76,800</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">76{,}800</annotation></semantics></math> frames). </span>(a)<span id="A1.F6.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Distribution of per-clip foreground fraction <math id="A1.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{fg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{fg}}</annotation></semantics></math>. </span>(b)<span id="A1.F6.7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Share of the <math id="A1.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(N^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(N^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> TRD budget consumed by each pair category (FG–FG, FG–BG, BG–BG) under vanilla TRD (uniform weighting) and the three routing operators (AND, OR, XOR). OR (ours) retains <math id="A1.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim\!70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo mathsize="1.110em">%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim\!70\%</annotation></semantics></math> of pairs by keeping all FG–FG and FG–BG pairs and discarding only BG–BG.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Stage 1 saliency aligner: ablation grid and quantitative analysis</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Quantitative metrics: definitions and rationale. ‣ A.3 Stage 1 saliency aligner: ablation grid and quantitative analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> ablates the predicted saliency <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> along the two Stage 1 design choices that matter for routing (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Stage 1: Text-conditioned saliency aligner ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, quantitative results in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Ablations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>): the entity-separated <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+2</annotation></semantics></math> supervision and the InfoNCE regulariser. Two held-out clips with distinct visual layouts confirm that the ranking is structural rather than scene-specific. Both <em id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o entity</em> rows saturate across nearly every patch and merge multiple subjects into a single foreground blob, the foreground-prior collapse that motivates entity-separated supervision. <em id="A1.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o NCE</em> is sharper but globally biased toward the dominant subject (the central musician in panel (a) and the front-row women in panel (b)) at the expense of smaller or more lateral entities. Only <em id="A1.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Full</em> produces a calibrated, per-entity response on every frame. Tab. <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.5 Stage 2 saliency on the full MTSS caption ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> quantifies these trends along four axes: saliency calibration (mean, max, coverage, entropy), cross-attention focus on text tokens, post-CA self-attention entropy gain, and PCA variance ratio.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantitative metrics: definitions and rationale.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each row of Tab. <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.5 Stage 2 saliency on the full MTSS caption ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is a scalar averaged over a held-out analysis set of <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=128</annotation></semantics></math> clips (<math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> frames per clip, disjoint from training). The seven metrics are computed from the predicted saliency <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}\in[0,1]^{N_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}\in[0,1]^{N_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>; the head-averaged cross-attention <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\Phi}_{\text{CA}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{v}\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>CA</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\Phi}_{\text{CA}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{v}\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> text tokens; the post-CA visual self-attention <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\text{post}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\text{post}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the raw V-JEPA self-attention <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\text{vj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>vj</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\text{vj}}</annotation></semantics></math>, both in <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{N_{h}\times N_{v}\times N_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{N_{h}\times N_{v}\times N_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> attention heads; and the visual features <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> (raw V-JEPA) vs. the post-CA, text-enhanced features <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}_{y}=\Phi_{\text{CA}}(V_{y},\tilde{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mtext>CA</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>E</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}_{y}=\Phi_{\text{CA}}(V_{y},\tilde{E})</annotation></semantics></math> of Sec. <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Stage 1: Text-conditioned saliency aligner ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>. Throughout, <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(p)=-\sum_{j}p_{j}\log p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(p)=-\sum_{j}p_{j}\log p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Shannon entropy of a probability vector <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, distinct from the Bernoulli entropy <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> defined below. Each metric is chosen to expose a specific failure mode visible in Fig. <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Quantitative metrics: definitions and rationale. ‣ A.3 Stage 1 saliency aligner: ablation grid and quantitative analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Saliency calibration</span> (probes foreground-prior collapse and mask binarisation):</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">saliency mean</em> <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\tfrac{1}{N_{v}}\sum_{n}M_{p}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\tfrac{1}{N_{v}}\sum_{n}M_{p}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Values close to the dataset’s foreground prior (<math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 0.6</annotation></semantics></math> for our <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=2</annotation></semantics></math> entities) indicate that the head fires on every patch.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:1.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">saliency max</em> <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\max_{n}M_{p}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\max_{n}M_{p}(n)</annotation></semantics></math>. Peaks <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to 1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to 1.0</annotation></semantics></math> mean the sigmoid has saturated and the head no longer outputs a graded signal.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:1.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">saliency coverage</em> <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="=\tfrac{1}{N_{v}}\sum_{n}\mathbf{1}[M_{p}(n)&gt;0.5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\tfrac{1}{N_{v}}\sum_{n}\mathbf{1}[M_{p}(n)&gt;0.5]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>-valued indicator. Fraction of patches above the decision threshold, where smaller values indicate selectivity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;padding-top:1.0pt;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">saliency entropy</em> <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\tfrac{1}{N_{v}}\sum_{n}H_{2}(M_{p}(n))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\tfrac{1}{N_{v}}\sum_{n}H_{2}(M_{p}(n))</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{2}(p)=-p\log p-(1-p)\log(1-p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{2}(p)=-p\log p-(1-p)\log(1-p)</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">High</span> entropy means each patch sits in a graded region near <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>, which is the regime the OR pair weight <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.4 Stage 2: Saliency-routed TRD ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) actually consumes. <span id="A1.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Low</span> entropy means <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> has been pushed to a hard <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> mask and <math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> collapses to an indicator. This row operationalises the “<math id="A1.I1.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a hard binary mask” claim of Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Stage 2: Saliency-routed TRD ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cross-attention focus</span> (probes whether CA actually consumes the caption):</p>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ca-focus mean</em> <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\tfrac{1}{N_{v}}\sum_{n}\bigl(1-H(\bar{\Phi}_{\text{CA}}(n,\cdot))/\log(L+1)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>CA</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\tfrac{1}{N_{v}}\sum_{n}\bigl(1-H(\bar{\Phi}_{\text{CA}}(n,\cdot))/\log(L+1)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\Phi}_{\text{CA}}(n,\cdot)\in\mathbb{R}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>CA</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>L</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\Phi}_{\text{CA}}(n,\cdot)\in\mathbb{R}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the cross-attention distribution of visual token <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> over the <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> text tokens. Higher values mean each visual query lands on a few specific text tokens rather than spreading uniformly, i.e. the saliency head is able to route by word identity.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Self-attention entropy gain</span> (probes whether CA enriches V-JEPA’s self-attention pattern or collapses it):</p>
<ul id="A1.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> self-attn entropy</em> <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=\bar{H}(A_{\text{post}})-\bar{H}(A_{\text{vj}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>post</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>vj</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=\bar{H}(A_{\text{post}})-\bar{H}(A_{\text{vj}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}(A)=\tfrac{1}{N_{h}N_{v}}\sum_{h,n}H(A^{(h)}_{n,\cdot})/\log N_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}(A)=\tfrac{1}{N_{h}N_{v}}\sum_{h,n}H(A^{(h)}_{n,\cdot})/\log N_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> averages the length-normalised Shannon entropy over the <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> heads and the <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> rows of each head’s attention matrix. <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{H}(A_{\text{vj}})=0.783" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>vj</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.783</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{H}(A_{\text{vj}})=0.783</annotation></semantics></math> on our analysis set. A clearly positive <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> means CA adds new spread on top of V-JEPA’s native attention, whereas a negative <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o NCE w/o entity</em>: <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.085" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.085</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.085</annotation></semantics></math>) means CA narrows the visual self-attention onto a single foreground blob, the spectral mirror of the foreground-prior collapse seen in <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Representation-rank preservation</span> (probes whether CA preserves V-JEPA’s high-rank structure):</p>
<ul id="A1.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A1.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">PCA <math id="A1.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> var. ratio</em> <math id="A1.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=r_{3}(V^{\prime}_{y})-r_{3}(V_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=r_{3}(V^{\prime}_{y})-r_{3}(V_{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{3}(V)=\sum_{i\leq 3}\sigma_{i}^{2}/\sum_{i}\sigma_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{3}(V)=\sum_{i\leq 3}\sigma_{i}^{2}/\sum_{i}\sigma_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the top-3 explained-variance ratio of the centred token matrix and <math id="A1.I4.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> its <math id="A1.I4.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th singular value (PCA via SVD). A small positive value means <math id="A1.I4.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> stays within V-JEPA’s rank profile, whereas a large positive value means CA has compressed the token cloud into a low-rank, foreground-only subspace, the spectral signature of the same collapse.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Read together, the seven rows are a conjunction: only <em id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Full</em> simultaneously avoids saturation (rows 1–3), keeps <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graded (row 4), focuses CA on entity-specific text tokens (row 5), widens rather than narrows V-JEPA self-attention (row 6), and preserves the rank profile of <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{y}</annotation></semantics></math> (row 7). Each ablation breaks at least one of these.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A1.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07800v1/ablation_saliency_grid1.png" id="A1.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/257;" width="476" height="257" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="A1.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Clip A: South Asian musicians playing instruments.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A1.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07800v1/ablation_saliency_grid2.png" id="A1.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/261;" width="476" height="261" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="A1.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Clip B: women in red ceremonial attire posing as a tightly grouped multi-subject foreground.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F7.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="A1.F7.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Stage 1 ablation on the predicted saliency map<span id="A1.F7.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> on two held-out clips, eight frames each. Rows: input frames; <em id="A1.F7.9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Full</em> (default SARA, <math id="A1.F7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K+2</annotation></semantics></math> forwards <math id="A1.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> InfoNCE); <em id="A1.F7.9.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o NCE</em>; <em id="A1.F7.9.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o NCE w/o entity</em> (single forward on full caption with union mask, no InfoNCE); <em id="A1.F7.9.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o entity</em> (single forward, InfoNCE retained). Saliency rows use a jet colormap: redder = higher saliency, bluer = lower. Discussion in App. <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Stage 1 saliency aligner: ablation grid and quantitative analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>, quantitative metrics in Tab. <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.5 Stage 2 saliency on the full MTSS caption ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Stage 1 saliency on per-entity queries</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ A.4 Stage 1 saliency on per-entity queries ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> fixes the trained Stage 1 aligner and sweeps the conditioning caption through the four supervision-time query types (two per-entity, one combined-entity, one background) on a held-out two-person clip. Three properties read off the figure: (i) panels (a)–(b) place <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> on <em id="A1.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">different</em> V-JEPA tokens for the two persons even though the subjects sit close together in the frame, so text-conditioned routing actually routes the saliency; (ii) the combined-entity panel (c) covers both subjects in a single response, behaving like a soft union of the per-entity panels; (iii) the background panel (d) cleanly inverts the foreground response, validating the complement supervision <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{bg}}=\mathbf{1}-M_{\text{fg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>bg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{bg}}=\mathbf{1}-M_{\text{fg}}</annotation></semantics></math> and showing the head can suppress, not only highlight, named regions on demand. The PCA row further indicates that the cross-attention <math id="A1.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{\text{CA}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mtext>CA</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{\text{CA}}</annotation></semantics></math> already organises features into entity-specific subspaces <em id="A1.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">before</em> the saliency head, so the head reads off, rather than re-discovers, this structure.</p>
</div>
