</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F8.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07800v1/stage1-case1-part-all-entities.png" id="A1.F8.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="A1.F8.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Query <math id="A1.F8.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{fg}}=[c_{1};c_{2};\dots]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{fg}}=[c_{1};c_{2};\dots]</annotation></semantics></math>: combined-entity.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A1.F8.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2605.07800v1/stage1-case1-part-background.png" id="A1.F8.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/228;" width="476" height="228" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="A1.F8.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Query <math id="A1.F8.sf4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\text{bg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mtext>bg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\text{bg}}</annotation></semantics></math>: background (cafeteria setting).</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F8.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="A1.F8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Stage 1 saliency on the four supervision-time query types<span id="A1.F8.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">, eight frames of one held-out clip. Rows in each panel: input frames, SAM 3.1 reference mask <math id="A1.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, PCA of <math id="A1.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, predicted saliency <math id="A1.F8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In 3.3 Stage 1: Text-conditioned saliency aligner ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>)). The <math id="A1.F8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> row uses a jet colormap: redder = higher saliency, bluer = lower. Discussion in App. <a href="#A1.SS4" title="A.4 Stage 1 saliency on per-entity queries ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.4</span></a>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2605.07800v1/stage2-mtss-infer-case1.png" id="A1.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/174;" width="405" height="174" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A1.F9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A1.F9.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Stage 2 saliency on the full MTSS caption.<span id="A1.F9.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Same aligner and clip as Fig. <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ A.4 Stage 1 saliency on per-entity queries ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, queried with the full MTSS string <math id="A1.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> that the VDM also consumes at TRD time. Rows: input frames, SAM 3.1 union mask <math id="A1.F9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{fg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>fg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{fg}}</annotation></semantics></math> (reference only, not used at Stage 2), PCA of <math id="A1.F9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{\prime}_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{\prime}_{y}</annotation></semantics></math>, predicted saliency <math id="A1.F9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <math id="A1.F9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> row uses a jet colormap: redder = higher saliency, bluer = lower. Note that <math id="A1.F9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A1.F9.7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> a hard binary mask: it places the highest values on the named subjects and <em id="A1.F9.7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adaptively</em> fades through intermediate values on nearby background to low values on far-field background, which is what the OR pair weight <math id="A1.F9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.4 Stage 2: Saliency-routed TRD ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) needs to grade, rather than just gate, each subject-background pair. Discussion in App. <a href="#A1.SS5" title="A.5 Stage 2 saliency on the full MTSS caption ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.5</span></a>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.5 </span>Stage 2 saliency on the full MTSS caption</h3>

<div id="A1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#A1.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ A.4 Stage 1 saliency on per-entity queries ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> switches the conditioning caption to the full MTSS string <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> (the same string the VDM is conditioned on at Stage 2 TRD time). Three facts about this figure carry SARA’s Stage 2 claim. (i) The aligner was <em id="A1.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">never</em> trained on the concatenated MTSS string, yet at inference it covers both subjects in a single coherent response that closely tracks the SAM-derived union mask. (ii) The response is clearly super-additive over the panels (a)–(b) of Fig. <a href="#A1.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ A.4 Stage 1 saliency on per-entity queries ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, so passing <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> through the OR pair weight <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.4 Stage 2: Saliency-routed TRD ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) amplifies subject-subject and subject-background pair supervision instead of attenuating it, the routing behaviour Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Stage 2: Saliency-routed TRD ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> promises. (iii) Critically, <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="A1.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> a hard binary foreground mask: the saliency head adaptively allocates weight between foreground and background, with the highest values concentrated on the two named subjects, intermediate values on background tokens that are spatially or semantically close to them (the part of the table the subjects sit at, the wall directly behind), and the smallest values on far-field background tokens (the periphery and the out-of-focus diners). This continuous allocation is exactly what the OR pair weight <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\vee}_{ij}=w_{i}+w_{j}-w_{i}w_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\vee}_{ij}=w_{i}+w_{j}-w_{i}w_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> needs in order to keep <em id="A1.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">all</em> subject-background pairs while still ranking them by how prompt-relevant the background endpoint is, instead of treating background as a single uniform region. A binary mask would collapse this gradient and reduce <math id="A1.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>∨</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math> to an indicator, which is exactly the regime SARA’s continuous-saliency design is meant to avoid (no thresholding, no floor, as in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Stage 2: Saliency-routed TRD ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span><span id="A1.T4.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage 1 ablation: quantitative metrics</span> (mean over <math id="A1.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=128</annotation></semantics></math> held-out clips, columns match the rows of Fig. <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Quantitative metrics: definitions and rationale. ‣ A.3 Stage 1 saliency aligner: ablation grid and quantitative analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, metric definitions in App. <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Stage 1 saliency aligner: ablation grid and quantitative analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>, p. <a href="#A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" title="Quantitative metrics: definitions and rationale. ‣ A.3 Stage 1 saliency aligner: ablation grid and quantitative analysis ‣ Appendix A Visualisations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>). <em id="A1.T4.14" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Full</em> is the only configuration that jointly avoids saliency saturation (rows 1–3), keeps <math id="A1.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> graded (row 4), focuses cross-attention on entity tokens (row 5), and enriches V-JEPA self-attention without collapsing it (rows 6–7), and each ablation fails on at least one axis.</figcaption>
