<td id="A4.T8.15.1.18.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.4pt 4.0pt;"><span id="A4.T8.15.1.18.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">48.51</span></td>
<td id="A4.T8.15.1.18.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.4pt 4.0pt;"><span id="A4.T8.15.1.18.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">44.17</span></td>
<td id="A4.T8.15.1.18.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.4pt 4.0pt;"><span id="A4.T8.15.1.18.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">53.44</span></td>
<td id="A4.T8.15.1.18.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.4pt 4.0pt;"><span id="A4.T8.15.1.18.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;">56.19</span></td>
<td id="A4.T8.15.1.18.7" class="ltx_td ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.4pt 4.0pt;"></td>
<td id="A4.T8.15.1.18.8" class="ltx_td ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.4pt 4.0pt;"></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Per-task scores.</span> Tab. <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ D.2 VBench-2.0 per-task scores ‣ Appendix D More results ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> matches the dimension-level summary of Tab. <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 VBench Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. SARA leads outright on <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Composition</em>, <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Dynamic Attribute</em>, and <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Camera Motion</em>, and ties for best on <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Motion Rationality</em> (with VideoREPA). These wins concentrate on the multi-entity-composition and dynamic-attribute axes that text-conditioned saliency routing directly targets. SFT posts the best score on five tasks—<em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Instance Preservation</em>, <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Complex Plot</em>, <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Material</em>, <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Multi-View Consistency</em>, and <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Thermotics</em>—driving its leading <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Physics</em> dimension score, though these per-task gains do not carry over to the Final score where SARA still leads. The pretrained Wan2.2 keeps a small lead on the three Human Fidelity tasks and on <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mechanics</em>, <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.13" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Dynamic Spatial Relationship</em>, and <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.14" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Motion Order Understanding</em>, the same high-noise-only-training trade-off observed on <span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">temporal_consistency</span> / <span id="A4.SS2.p2.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">physical_plausibility</span> (App. <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C VLM-rubric evaluation protocol ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>, App. <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Limitations and broader impact ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>): all continually-trained rows update only the high-noise expert of Wan2.2’s two-expert MoE while the low-noise expert (which renders anatomy and fine textures) stays frozen, so any high-noise update propagates as a small distribution shift on the un-updated low-noise expert. SARA shows the smallest such drop among the continually-trained methods. MoAlign wins on <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.17" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Diversity</em> and <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.18" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Complex Landscape</em>, while VideoREPA leads on <em id="A4.SS2.p2.1.19" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Human Interaction</em>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Additional ablations</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>DiT alignment hookup layer</h3>

<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Stage 2 masked TRD loss of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.4 Stage 2: Saliency-routed TRD ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is computed on the projected hidden state <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> of a single Wan2.2 DiT layer. The Wan2.2 high-noise DiT has <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> layers, and throughout the main paper we hook the alignment loss into layer <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> (mid-depth, see Tab. <a href="#A6.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ Appendix F Training details ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). Tab. <a href="#A5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ E.1 DiT alignment hookup layer ‣ Appendix E Additional ablations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> ablates this choice by moving the hookup to deeper layers (<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>36</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>39</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39</annotation></semantics></math>), holding every other component fixed at the default SARA configuration (saliency aligner, OR pair fusion, entity-separated supervision, InfoNCE, <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, optimisation schedule, <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math>K-clip training corpus, <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> steps), so that only the hookup layer changes. Layer <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> as the default is well-supported by prior REPA-family work on shallower DiTs: VideoREPA on CogVideoX <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> and MoAlign on Wan2.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> both find layer <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> the empirical optimum in their published layer sweeps. Wan2.2 widens this depth budget to <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> layers, so the open question for our setting is whether the optimum shifts deeper to track the larger DiT (e.g. to a comparable fractional depth around layer <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>24</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">24</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math>). Tab. <a href="#A5.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ E.1 DiT alignment hookup layer ‣ Appendix E Additional ablations ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> verifies it does not, with layer <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> still dominating over <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>36</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>39</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39</annotation></semantics></math>. We do not re-test shallower hookups because REPA’s original layer sweep on DiT-XL/2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> established that pre-mid-depth blocks carry predominantly low-level and positional signal and underperform mid-depth alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The mid-depth hookup at layer <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> dominates on all four VLM-rubric metrics, and every deeper hookup (<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>36</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>39</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39</annotation></semantics></math>) underperforms it on every column, with the gap up to <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.055" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.055</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.055</annotation></semantics></math> on TA mean and <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.039" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.039</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.039</annotation></semantics></math> on MQ mean. Among the deeper hookups themselves the differences are small (<math id="A5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 0.04</annotation></semantics></math> on TA mean and <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 0.03</annotation></semantics></math> on MQ mean) and non-monotonic in the layer index (TA mean and TA vote in fact rise from layer <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>39</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39</annotation></semantics></math> while MQ shows a U-shape that dips at layer <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="36" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>36</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">36</annotation></semantics></math> and recovers at <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>39</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39</annotation></semantics></math>), so the operative axis is mid-depth vs late-depth rather than the precise late-layer position. One plausible reason: mid-depth blocks in large DiTs still carry spatially localised but semantically structured tokens, whereas the latest layers have specialised toward noise prediction and are less compatible with pair-relational alignment against the V-JEPA target. We therefore use layer <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>18</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18</annotation></semantics></math> for Wan2.2 throughout the main-paper experiments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A5.T9.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 9</span>: </span><span id="A5.T9.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Effect of the DiT alignment hookup layer on Wan2.2 high-noise<span id="A5.T9.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (<math id="A5.T9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math> DiT layers total). Each row retrains SARA with the masked TRD loss of Eq. (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.4 Stage 2: Saliency-routed TRD ‣ 3 Method ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) attached to a different layer, with all other settings matching the default SARA configuration. Metrics follow the VLM rubric of Tab. <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main comparison ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SARA: Semantically Adaptive Relational Alignment for Video Diffusion Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Best in </span>bold<span id="A5.T9.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">.</span></span></figcaption>
